recommend check-in (Keaton) -- the bench is occupied and %281 is the reason, now three firings deep and caught at the door this time rather than out of a false RED. Two sessions stand in ~/grain: pid 2240 on claude-fable-5 since 02:20:52, and mine since 02:23:10. Your word picks the answer, and both are still the two the row named: a build lock or a per-process shadow name inside bridge_to_zig, which is a seam in Rye's own driver, or one working tree per star, which is how the three aether stars are run rather than any code. Ungated and fully surveyed for whichever bench takes it next: nineteen .rye files carry BOTH legs of %282, not the one the last recommend named -- the x86-64 socket column AND a hand-rolled Linux sockaddr_in -- and std.c already publishes both correctly per host, so the repair is to delete nineteen copies of a table rather than to write a twentieth. Each of the nineteen has a tool that names it, so each closes on a witness.
